      "test_retry_idempotency.py": {
        "kind": "invariant",
        "rationale": "Change A retries charge() after a GatewayTimeout, but it builds the idempotency key inside the retry loop. Change B makes idempotency_key() return a new key on every call. After the merge, a timeout whose response was lost ('timeout_after') is retried under a new key, and the gateway captures the money a second time. On base and B there is no retry, and on A the retry reuses the key and is deduplicated. So at most one capture, and one key per charge() call, should hold on every tree except the merge."
      },
      "test_retry_lost_response.py": {
        "kind": "intent_a",
        "rationale": "Change A says a timeout that would succeed on a second try should not fail checkout. When the first response is lost after the gateway captured ('timeout_after'), the retry should return that same capture and should not create a new one. With B's per-call keys, the retry creates a second capture, so this check fails on the merge."
      },
      "test_fresh_keys_after_decline.py": {
        "kind": "intent_b",
        "rationale": "Change B lets a customer retry checkout after a decline, because each charge() call sends a key the gateway has not seen. This uses a fake gateway that remembers a decline per key. A second charge() call for the same order should succeed. Separate charge() calls should send different keys, and each key should still name the order."
      }
    },
